# routing.fk: small pieces that help the router, yours to change. Each one is a sealed module: its
# ports are where belts cross its edge, and its one net is laid inside it. Place one where you want
# a belt to go and run your net through it; the router lays each stretch as a net of its own.
#
#   import Waypoint, Merge, Split from "routing.fk"
#
# A net the router can't find a way for: make it pass a spot of your choosing.
#
#   wp = Waypoint<item=iron-gear-wheel> @(8, 28)
#   net gear: gears -> wp -> red, belts, ins
#
# A chain like this is still one net, gear. Its stretches are gear (gears to the waypoint) and
# gear@wp (the waypoint on), and the route step and fk status show them together. A piece in a
# chain has inout ports: the first one is its way in, the others its ways out.
#
# One belt into two, where you want the split. The consumers after it are shared, the first to
# way a, the second to way b; or take a way on to consumers of its own on the next line:
#
#   sp = Split<item=copper-plate> @(4, 16)
#   net copper: copper_smelt -> sp -> cable, red     (cable on a, red on b)
#
#   net copper: copper_smelt -> sp -> cable
#     sp.b -> wp2 -> red                             (b goes by waypoint wp2)
#
# The same written as plain nets, one per stretch, also works (wp.i and wp.o name the ports):
#
#   net gear:  gears -> wp.i
#   net gear2: wp.o -> red, belts, ins
#
# Two belts into one, where you want the join:
#
#   mg = Merge<a=automation-science-pack, b=logistic-science-pack> @(4, 40)
#   net pk_r:  red -> mg.a
#   net pk_g:  green -> mg.b
#   net packs: mg.o -> labs
#
# A Merge joins two nets, so it is not in a chain: its ports are in and out.
#
# Each piece runs north to south (in at its top edge, out at its bottom); turn it with `rot`
# (@(x, y, rot 1) runs east to west, rot 3 west to east). Make your own the same way: ports on
# the edge, one net between them, as long or as wide as you like. docs\LANGUAGE.md, "Modules
# with ports", has the rules.

# One belt, three tiles long: the net passes here.
module Waypoint<item> size 1x3 {
  port i : inout $item @n0
  port o : inout $item @s0
  net p: i -> o
}

# Two belts into one, by a splitter. A splitter keeps lanes: each item leaves on the lane it came
# in on, so this does not put a and b on lanes of their own (`lanes`, in LANGUAGE.md, does).
module Merge<a, b> size 2x3 {
  port a : in  $a @n0
  port b : in  $b @n1
  port o : out $a | $b @s0
  net p: a, b -> o
}

# One belt into two, by a splitter.
module Split<item> size 2x3 {
  port i : inout $item @n0
  port a : inout $item @s0
  port b : inout $item @s1
  net p: i -> a, b
}

# A pump in a pipe, 1x2: `net lub: oil -> pp -> engines` with pp = InlinePump<fluid=lubricant>.
# A long pipe loses throughput; a pump starts a new run, so the route step's pipe-length check
# names where one helps. (Pump<fluid=...> is the one pump on its own, for nets written by hand.)
module InlinePump<fluid> size 1x2 {
  port i : inout $fluid @n0
  port o : inout $fluid @s0
  pm = Pump<fluid=$fluid> @(0, 0, rot 2)
  net a: i -> pm
  net b: pm -> o
}
